<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Keychain access group for Firebase Auth.

	  Firebase Auth persists the signed-in session in the iOS Keychain. Without
	  an explicit access group the app relies on the implicit default, which is
	  derived from the team prefix and bundle identifier — and BOTH of those
	  changed on 2026-08-17 (bundle com.cigarloungeapp.app ->
	  com.enteraxion.thereserve, team -> Enteraxion 9MSD728TMT). On a real
	  device that left the app unable to reach its own keychain items, and every
	  sign-in failed with `auth/keychain-error` while the credentials themselves
	  were perfectly valid — the account authenticated fine against Firebase
	  directly.

	  Declaring the group explicitly is what Firebase's iOS setup expects, and
	  it means a future bundle-id or team change is a one-line edit here rather
	  than a mystery sign-in failure.

	  Both halves are build settings rather than literals on purpose:
	  $(AppIdentifierPrefix) expands to the team ID and $(CFBundleIdentifier) to
	  the bundle id, so this is always the app's own default access group. That
	  matters because hardcoding either one is what turns the *next* bundle-id
	  or team change back into this same mystery.
	-->
	<!--
	  Push notifications. `development` here is the APNs *environment* the app
	  talks to, not a build configuration — Xcode rewrites it to `production`
	  when archiving for TestFlight or the App Store, which is why both APNs
	  auth key slots are filled in the Firebase console (2026-09-13). Leave this
	  as development; overriding it to production breaks push in local builds.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
